= Dang Gui (Chinese Angelica Root)

#emph[Scope: This entry treats #emph[dang gui] (#emph[dāngguī], 當歸) — #emph[Angelica sinensis] (Oliv.) Diels, family Apiaceae — the canonical Chinese 當歸 of the #emph[Shennong Bencao Jing] and the foundational #emph[blood-tonifying drug of Chinese medicine], traditionally called the #emph["Queen of Women's Herbs"] (婦科聖藥 fùkē shèngyào). The closely-related Japanese #emph[tōki] (当帰) is #emph[Angelica acutiloba] Kitag., treated in the Japanese kampō tradition as a distinct but functionally equivalent drug; the #emph[European garden angelica] (#emph[Angelica archangelica]) is #emph[different] and is treated separately in #emph[angelica-root.typ]. Status: classical in Chinese medicine (top grade in #emph[Shennong Bencao Jing]); central in Korean (당귀 danggwi) and Japanese kampō (当帰 tōki) traditions; not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions. Modern Western and global use as a women's-health botanical, with substantial recent phytopharmacology around ligustilide and the angelica polysaccharides.]

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Angelica sinensis_ (Oliv.) Diels, family Apiaceae (Umbelliferae); the drug is the *dried root* (_Angelicae sinensis radix_), harvested at 2–3 years from cultivated stock, typically from Gansu, Yunnan, and Sichuan provinces (the *Min County 岷縣* district of Gansu producing the most prized material). The species epithet *sinensis* — "Chinese" — distinguishes the Chinese species from the European garden angelica (_A. archangelica_) and the Japanese (_A. acutiloba_). The genus and family etymology is covered in `angelica-root.typ` (the medieval Latin _angelica_ from Late Latin _angelicus_ "angelic, of the angel").

*Chinese:* *當歸 (dāngguī)* — the canonical Chinese drug name, recorded in the *Shennong Bencao Jing* (神農本草經, Han dynasty) as a *top-grade drug (上品)*. The character 當 dāng = "should, ought, must"; the character 歸 guī = "return, come back home." The compound 當歸 literally "*should return*" — and the *etymology of the name is itself classical*, with several traditional readings preserved by Li Shizhen and other commentators:
- *Blood-returning reading:* the herb is given to women whose blood is depleted or stagnant — *the blood should return* to its proper place / circulation. This is the canonical pharmacological reading.
- *Husband-returning reading:* a folk-traditional reading — the herb is taken by women whose husbands are away (military, trade, official duty), expressing the wish that the *husband should return*. This reading is recorded by Li Shizhen and reflects the herb's intimate association with women's health and the Confucian family-and-affective context.
- *Combined symbolic reading:* the herb is the symbol of *restoration to proper place* — blood, husband, health, balance.

The drug is conventionally divided into named parts with subtly different actions:
- *當歸頭 (dāngguī tóu, "dang gui head")* — the *upper / head* portion of the root; emphasizes *blood-tonifying* (補血).
- *當歸身 (dāngguī shēn, "dang gui body")* — the *body / middle* portion; *balanced* between tonifying and moving.
- *當歸尾 (dāngguī wěi, "dang gui tail")* — the *lower / tail* (lateral rootlets); emphasizes *blood-moving* (活血).
- *全當歸 (quán dāngguī, "whole dang gui")* — the whole root, used when both actions are desired.

The fine-grained classical *parts-of-the-root* refinement is one of the more distinctive features of dang gui clinical use — the herb's actions are dose- and part-dependent, an unusually elaborated classical pharmacology.#footnote[當歸 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (top grade); Li Shizhen, _Bencao Gangmu_, 1578 — extensive entry with the etymological discussion; Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. The parts-of-root distinctions are preserved in classical and modern Chinese pharmacy.]

*English:* *Dang gui* (the pinyin transliteration, used in modern academic and herbal literature); *Dong quai* (the older Wade-Giles spelling, common in Western herbal commerce); *Chinese angelica*, *Chinese angelica root*; *female ginseng* (a Western marketing term, sometimes used but not classical).

*Korean:* *당귀 (danggwi)* — Korean reading of the same characters; the Korean tradition uses 당귀 in many compound preparations, particularly women's and convalescent formulas. The Korean cultivation is principally _A. gigas_ (참당귀 _chamdanggwi_), a distinct Korean species — *taxonomically different from the Chinese A. sinensis but used in parallel clinical register*. The species distinction matters in modern Chinese-Korean pharmaceutical trade.

*Japanese:* *当帰 (tōki)* — Japanese reading; the Japanese kampō uses *_Angelica acutiloba_* Kitag. (Japanese angelica) rather than _A. sinensis_ — a *taxonomically distinct species* but used in parallel clinical register. The Yamato and Hokkaido cultivation traditions are preserved.

*Sanskrit / Arabic / Latin classical:* *Not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions.* The Chinese A. sinensis is an East Asian temperate herb outside the canonical reach of those traditions; for cross-system discussion see `angelica-root.typ` (which covers the European A. archangelica and its Tibb / Greco-Roman / Ayurvedic-by-extension treatment).

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*當歸 is one of the most canonical and most clinically central Chinese drugs* — top-grade in the *Shennong Bencao Jing*, the *foundational blood-tonifying drug* of the Chinese tradition, and the *signature drug of Chinese women's medicine* (婦科). The classical title *"婦科聖藥" (fùkē shèngyào, "sage drug of women's medicine")* captures its status. Used in countless classical formulas; arguably no other herb is as central to Chinese gynecology.#footnote[當歸 in _Shennong Bencao Jing_ (top grade); _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; classical Chinese gynecology literature (Fu Qingzhu, _Fu Qing Zhu Nü Ke_).]

*Property (性 xìng / 氣 qì):* Warm (溫).

*Flavor (味 wèi):* Sweet (甘), Pungent (辛), slightly Bitter (苦).

*Channels entered (歸經 guījīng):* Liver (肝), Heart (心), Spleen (脾).

*Actions & indications:*
- *補血 (bǔ xuè)* — *tonifies blood* — the cardinal action; for blood vacuity with pale complexion, dizziness, palpitations, dry skin, blurred vision, scanty or absent menstruation, postpartum debility. The *head and body* portions are emphasized for this action.
- *活血 (huó xuè)* — *moves blood* — for blood stasis with fixed pain, dysmenorrhea (especially with dark clots), traumatic injury, post-stroke conditions. The *tail* portion is emphasized for this action.
- *調經 (tiáo jīng)* — *regulates menstruation* — the combined blood-tonifying and blood-moving action makes 當歸 the canonical menstrual-regulator for *both* delayed/scanty menstruation (blood vacuity) and *painful/dark-clotted* menstruation (blood stasis); the herb is unusually clinically flexible across the spectrum of menstrual irregularities.
- *止痛 (zhǐ tòng)* — *stops pain* — particularly *blood-vacuity or blood-stasis pain* — abdominal pain, headache, joint pain from chronic blood vacuity; combined with cinnamon and peony in classical formulations.
- *潤腸通便 (rùn cháng tōng biàn)* — *moistens the intestines, promotes bowel movement* — for constipation from blood vacuity (dry, hard, infrequent stools with weakness, pale complexion); a *gentle laxative for vacuity patterns*, unlike the harsh purgatives used for excess-pattern constipation.
- *補心血、安神 (bǔ xīn xuè, ān shén)* — *tonifies Heart blood, calms spirit* — for palpitations, insomnia, anxiety from Heart-blood vacuity.
- *Modern oncology and chemotherapy-support register:* 當歸 polysaccharides are extensively used in modern Chinese clinical oncology for chemotherapy-induced anemia, leukopenia, and bone-marrow recovery support; the herb's *blood-tonifying* action extends naturally into the modern hematological-recovery register.

*Cautions:* excessive use causes *abdominal distension and loose stools* (the warming-moistening action can overwhelm Spleen-Stomach in damp-pattern patients); damp-heat patterns; food stagnation; pregnancy at high doses or with strong blood-moving formulas (the blood-moving action contraindicates large doses in pregnancy, though small supportive doses are used in classical postpartum and convalescent care). *Diarrhea, bleeding disorders, and acute infections with strong heat* are classical contraindications.#footnote[Actions, parts-of-root distinctions, and cautions: 當歸 in _Bencao Gangmu_; Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; classical Chinese gynecology literature.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Dang gui is not classical in Ayurveda._ The reading by extension would place the herb in the *vāta-pitta-pacifying* register — warming-sweet-snigdha for blood-and-tissue tonification; the *rasāyana* class (rejuvenating). Modern post-classical Ayurvedic integrative use of dang gui alongside classical Ayurvedic women's-medicine herbs (śatāvarī, aśoka, lodhra) — *post-classical extension only*, flagged.

For the Indian classical-Sanskrit related identifications and the (contested) Himalayan _Angelica glauca_ = _coraka_ reading, see `angelica-root.typ`.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Dang gui is not classical in Tibb._ No Avicennan, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Tibb al-Nabawī attestation. For the post-classical Tibb placement of the European A. archangelica, see `angelica-root.typ` (the Chinese A. sinensis is a different species and the European-tradition placement does not transfer cleanly).

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

*The cornerstone formulas of Chinese women's medicine.*

- *四物湯 (Sì Wù Tāng, "Four Substances Decoction")* — the *foundational blood-tonifying formula*: 當歸 + 川芎 (chuānxiōng, Ligusticum) + 白芍 (báisháo, white peony) + 熟地黃 (shú dì huáng, prepared rehmannia). From _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_ (12th c.). *The most-prescribed blood-tonic formula in Chinese medicine*; the basis of dozens of derivative formulas across gynecology, hematology, and convalescent care. Modern Chinese clinical use is extensive; the formula is the *Chinese-medical archetype* of the blood-tonifying intervention.
- *當歸補血湯 (Dāng Guī Bǔ Xuè Tāng, "Dang Gui Tonify-Blood Decoction")* — Li Dongyuan; 黃芪 (astragalus) : 當歸 in a *5:1 ratio*; for severe qi-blood vacuity. The classical demonstration of *qi-generates-blood* — the principal herb by weight is astragalus, not dang gui. See `astragalus.typ`.
- *當歸四逆湯 (Dāng Guī Sì Nì Tāng, "Dang Gui Counterflow Reverse Decoction")* — Zhang Zhongjing, _Shanghan Lun_; 當歸 + 桂枝 + 芍藥 + 細辛 + 通草 + 大棗 + 甘草; for blood-vacuity with cold causing *cold extremities* — modern correspondence to Raynaud-type peripheral vascular insufficiency, peripheral neuropathy with cold pattern. One of the great Han-dynasty classical formulas.
- *桃紅四物湯 (Táo Hóng Sì Wù Tāng, "Peach-and-Safflower Four Substances Decoction")* — 四物湯 + 桃仁 (peach kernel) + 紅花 (safflower); for blood vacuity with stasis, particularly menstrual disorders with clotting.
- *逍遙散 (Xiāo Yáo Sǎn, "Free and Easy Wanderer Powder")* — _Tài Píng Huì Mín Hé Jì Jú Fāng_; 當歸 + 柴胡 + 白芍 + 白朮 + 茯苓 + 甘草 + 生薑 + 薄荷; for Liver-qi constraint with blood vacuity and Spleen weakness, especially the women's *premenstrual-and-mood* syndrome. *One of the most-prescribed Chinese formulas globally*, including in modern integrative gynecology.
- *當歸生薑羊肉湯 (Dāng Guī Shēng Jiāng Yáng Ròu Tāng, "Dang Gui, Fresh Ginger, and Lamb Decoction")* — _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; 當歸 + 生薑 + 羊肉; for postpartum and convalescent vacuity-cold with abdominal pain. The *foundational Chinese postpartum-care food-medicine formula* — eaten in the traditional *zuòyuèzi (坐月子, "sitting the month")* postpartum recovery regimen.
- *補陽還五湯 (Bǔ Yáng Huán Wǔ Tāng, "Tonify Yang and Restore Five Tenths Decoction")* — Wang Qingren, _Yi Lin Gai Cuo_; 黃芪 (very large dose) + 當歸尾 + 赤芍 + 川芎 + 桃仁 + 紅花 + 地龍; for post-stroke hemiplegia with qi vacuity blood stasis. See `astragalus.typ`.
- *八珍湯 (Bā Zhēn Tāng, "Eight Treasures Decoction")* — 四物湯 + 四君子湯 (the qi-tonifying counterpart); for combined qi-and-blood vacuity. *The cornerstone of post-illness, post-surgical, and post-childbirth recovery formulas*.
- *十全大補湯 (Shí Quán Dà Bǔ Tāng, "Ten Complete Great Tonifying Decoction")* — 八珍湯 + 黃芪 + 肉桂; for severe qi-and-blood vacuity with cold pattern.
- *人參養榮湯 (Rén Shēn Yǎng Róng Tāng, "Ginseng Nourishing-Construction Decoction")* — for severe qi-blood-yin vacuity with consumption.
- *當歸 in postpartum care, menstrual disorders, infertility regimens, anemia, dysmenorrhea, amenorrhea* — countless modern Chinese formulas use 當歸 across the women's-health spectrum.
- *Dietetic preparations:* 當歸生薑羊肉湯 (as above), 當歸雞 (dang gui chicken), 當歸紅棗茶 (dang gui jujube tea), 當歸藥膳 (dang gui medicinal-meal preparations).#footnote[Formula sources: Bensky & Barolet, _Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed.; Zhang Zhongjing, _Shanghan Lun_ and _Jin Gui Yao Lue_; _Tai Ping Hui Min He Ji Ju Fang_; Li Dongyuan, _Pi Wei Lun_; Wang Qingren, _Yi Lin Gai Cuo_; Fu Qingzhu, _Fu Qing Zhu Nü Ke_ — classical Chinese gynecology. The clinical center of gravity for 當歸 is women's medicine; the formula coverage in this single drug is among the broadest in the materia medica.]

=== Korean (당귀 danggwi)

- *Numerous Korean compound formulas* in the women's-medicine, postpartum, and convalescent registers, using primarily _A. gigas_ (the Korean species) rather than _A. sinensis_; the formula architecture is parallel to Chinese 當歸 use.
- *당귀차 (danggwi-cha)* — Korean dang gui tea, taken as a tonic for women.

=== Japanese kampō (当帰 tōki)

- *Tōki-shakuyaku-san (当帰芍薬散)* — the famous Japanese kampō formula for women's circulation, menstrual irregularities, infertility; one of the most-prescribed Japanese kampō preparations.
- Many other kampō formulas use 当帰 (_A. acutiloba_).

=== Ayurveda, Tibb, Greco-Roman

No classical formulas.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Dang gui is a *cool-temperate continental* perennial — native to the *cool-mountainous regions of Gansu, Yunnan, Sichuan, Shanxi, and the broader Northwest-and-Central Chinese highland*. The plant requires cool summers, cold winters, and moist mountain conditions; the *Min County (岷縣) of Gansu* — at ~2200 m elevation in the eastern Tibetan-plateau fringe — produces the most prized commercial dang gui in the world, with the Min-County designation comparable to Champagne or Parma as a quality-and-origin indicator. See `climates.typ` for the foundational framework.

=== Cool-temperate continental highland (the home climate)

Cool-cold winters; cool-moist summers; constitutional cool-moist pressure on Vāta-Kapha. The classical cultivation pattern is *autumn-harvest of two-to-three-year-old roots, sun-drying or low-heat drying, then sulfur-fumigation for storage* — though sulfur fumigation is controversial in modern commerce (residue issues).

*Traditional preparation.* Chinese-medical use is *decoctional and pharmaceutical*; the Chinese postpartum dietetic register uses 當歸 in *chicken or lamb stews* with ginger as a warming-blood-tonifying-snigdha preparation — the cornerstone food-medicine combination, climatically apt for cold winter convalescence in cool-continental climates.

*Cares.* The warming-sweet-snigdha-blood-tonifying register is universally applicable across climates but most climatically intuitive in cool-and-cold-season contexts; hot-summer or hot-humid use should be moderated (the warming-snigdha action can compound damp-heat in tropical climates).

=== Other climates — post-classical use

Modern Western use of dang gui is climatically agnostic. The classical Chinese register anchors to women's-medicine and postpartum care across all climates, though the cool-season pairing is preferred in classical dietetic practice.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern Western "dong quai for menopause / PMS / hormone balance" supplement register has globalized dang gui use across all climates and constitutional types; the high-dose isolated-extract preparations may compound the warming-snigdha-moving profile in hot-Pitta or summer contexts. The classical Chinese register pairs dang gui *within compound formulas* (not as a single-herb supplement) — providing constitutional balance through formula architecture. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the continental seasonal-extreme profile.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (dried root):*
- *Volatile oil (~0.4–0.7%):* *ligustilide* (the principal compound, 30–60% of the oil — *the canonical pharmacological marker*), butylidenephthalide, butylphthalide, sedanonic acid, other phthalides. Ligustilide is *unstable* — degrades on exposure to air, heat, and light — making fresh, properly stored dang gui pharmacologically superior to old material.
- *Polysaccharides (5–15%)* — *Angelica sinensis polysaccharides (ASP)*; high molecular weight; the principal *immunomodulatory and hematopoietic* fraction; multiple subfractions with documented effects on bone marrow function, RBC and WBC generation, and immune support.
- *Ferulic acid* — phenolic acid; the *pharmacognosy reference compound* alongside ligustilide; anti-inflammatory, antioxidant, anticoagulant, antispasmodic.
- *Coumarins:* angelicone, scopoletin, umbelliferone, osthol.
- *Phytosterols:* β-sitosterol, stigmasterol, daucosterol.
- *Vitamin B12:* dang gui has historically been claimed as a plant source of B12 — *the claim is dubious* and likely reflects analytical artifacts; the modern reading does not treat dang gui as a meaningful B12 source.
- *Trace amounts of vitamins (A, E), modest minerals.*#footnote[Phytochemistry: extensive modern pharmacognosy; standard references in _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_, _Phytochemistry_, _Planta Medica_; ligustilide and ferulic acid are the principal pharmacognosy markers; polysaccharide fractions are well-characterized. The B12 claim in older Western herbal literature is not well-supported by modern analytical chemistry and should be treated with skepticism.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Hematopoietic / anti-anemic — the headline modern action*. ASP polysaccharides have documented effects on bone marrow function, RBC generation, and recovery from chemotherapy-induced cytopenia; ferulic acid additionally supports erythropoiesis. Modern Chinese clinical use in oncology for chemotherapy-induced anemia, leukopenia, and bone-marrow recovery is substantial; clinical evidence is moderately supportive.
- *Vascular / circulatory* — ligustilide has *significant vasodilator activity*; ferulic acid has antiplatelet effect; clinical use in Chinese cardiology and stroke rehabilitation; some Western interest in peripheral circulation and cognitive function.
- *Smooth muscle effects* — *biphasic uterine activity*: low doses *relax* uterine smooth muscle (used for dysmenorrhea); high doses *contract* uterine smooth muscle (the basis of the *pregnancy contraindication for high doses*; classical postpartum uses depend on this latter action).
- *Phytoestrogenic (modest)* — dang gui has been investigated as a menopausal-symptom herb; the *clinical evidence is mixed-to-negative*: a high-quality 1997 RCT (Hirata et al.) showed dang gui *alone* was *not effective* for menopausal hot flashes; subsequent studies have found similar results. *The popular Western "dong quai for menopause" reputation substantially exceeds the evidence* — flagged. Within the classical Chinese formulary, dang gui is *used in compound formulas* for women's-health indications, not as a standalone for menopausal symptoms specifically.
- *Anti-inflammatory* — preclinical evidence; ferulic acid and ligustilide both contribute.
- *Antispasmodic* — ligustilide is a smooth-muscle relaxant; basis of the antispasmodic action.
- *Anticoagulant / antiplatelet* — modest; ferulic acid and coumarins; *significant interaction with warfarin and other anticoagulants* — well-documented in case reports.
- *Anti-tumor (preclinical)* — promising preclinical data; clinical translation preliminary.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Chinese decoction:* 6–15 g per day in standard practice; up to 30 g per day in compound formulas for specific indications.
- *Standardized extract:* 250–500 mg per day (Western herbalism).
- *Tincture:* 1–4 mL of 1:5 tincture, 2–3 times daily.
- *Pregnancy:* *avoid high therapeutic doses* — the high-dose uterine-stimulant effect contraindicates pregnancy use; small doses in postpartum and convalescent care are the classical register, not pregnancy use.
- *Anticoagulant interaction:* *significant clinical concern* — *avoid concurrent use with warfarin, heparin, NOACs without close INR monitoring*; case reports of bleeding episodes with dang gui + warfarin are documented.
- *Antiplatelet medications:* additive effect; monitor.
- *Pre-surgical:* discontinue 2 weeks before surgery (bleeding risk).
- *Hormone-sensitive cancers* (breast, ovarian, uterine): the phytoestrogenic activity is modest, but the popular use as a women's-health botanical raises the question; clinical evidence does not strongly support dang gui's estrogenic action, but caution is reasonable in active hormone-sensitive cancer contexts.
- *Diarrhea and bleeding patterns:* classical contraindications preserved.
- *Acute febrile illness:* classical contraindication.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.* The anticoagulant interaction is the principal modern pharmacokinetic concern requiring clinician supervision.#footnote[Dose ranges: Bensky et al., _Materia Medica_, 3rd ed.; AHP _Dang Gui Monograph_. Anticoagulant interaction: documented in case reports and standard interaction references; clinical caveat for warfarin patients is real and substantial. Menopausal symptom evidence: Hirata et al., _Fertility and Sterility_ 1997 — negative trial of dang gui monotherapy for hot flashes; subsequent reviews confirm modest-to-negative effect for menopausal symptoms in monotherapy use (which is *not* the classical formulary context).]

